# That every read follows a store is a fact of the order alone. A variable that is expected to hold a number and is handed a value nobody knows stays expected, and still loses the dead zone:
# its slot holds anything, never `absent`, so a read tests the value once and asks nothing about initialization.
# A variable that is read before its store keeps the dead zone.
# RUN: valueflow-apply
# CHECK: variable g0 name="early" {{*}}declared={type=number flags=[IsExpected]} flow={writers=AllKnown {{*}}initialization=StoredBeforeEveryRead
# CHECK: variable g1 name="late" {{*}}declared={type=number|absent flags=[IsExpected]} flow={writers=AllKnown
# CHECK-NOT: initialization
# CHECK: function f0

module m0 path="main.js" displayPath="main.js" body=f0
entry module=m0
variable g0 name="early" module=m0 declared={type=number|absent flags=[IsExpected]} initialization=Uninitialized
variable g1 name="late" module=m0 declared={type=number|absent flags=[IsExpected]} initialization=Uninitialized
function f0 name="main" kind=Module results=[{type=anything}] {
  b0():
    v0 = Builtin builtin=GlobalThis
    v1 = PropGet v0 key="input" [isFreeName]
    PutModuleVariable v1 variable=g0
    v3 = NewClosure function=f1
    v4 = Call v3
    PutModuleVariable v1 variable=g1
    Return v4
}
function f1 name="reader" results=[{type=anything}] {
  b0():
    v0 = GetModuleVariable variable=g0
    v1 = CheckInitialized v0 name="early"
    v2 = GetModuleVariable variable=g1
    v3 = CheckInitialized v2 name="late"
    Return v1
}
